STAT(2)                        P32W Unix                         STAT(2)

NAME
       stat, fstat - get file status

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/stat.h>

       int stat(const char *path, struct stat *st);
       int fstat(int fd, struct stat *st);

DESCRIPTION
       stat fills *st with information about the file path; fstat
       does the same for the open file fd. No permission on the
       file itself is needed, only search permission on the
       directories in path.

              struct stat {
                  int st_dev;              /* its filesystem's device */
                  unsigned st_ino;         /* inode number */
                  unsigned short st_mode;  /* type and permissions */
                  unsigned short st_nlink; /* number of links */
                  unsigned short st_uid;   /* owner */
                  unsigned short st_gid;   /* group */
                  int st_rdev;             /* device number (devices) */
                  int st_size;             /* size in bytes */
                  int st_atime;            /* = st_mtime */
                  int st_mtime;            /* last modification */
                  int st_ctime;            /* last status change */
              };

       Times are seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC. st_dev and
       st_rdev are device numbers, major * 256 + minor; st_rdev is
       the device a special file stands for.

       The type in st_mode (st_mode & S_IFMT) is one of

       S_IFREG  0100000  regular file
       S_IFDIR  0040000  directory
       S_IFCHR  0020000  character device
       S_IFBLK  0060000  block device
       S_IFIFO  0010000  pipe (from fstat only)

       and S_ISREG(m), S_ISDIR(m), S_ISCHR(m) and S_ISBLK(m) test
       for the first four. The other bits are those of chmod(2):
       S_ISUID 04000, S_ISGID 02000, S_ISVTX 01000 and the nine
       permission bits.

       For a pipe, fstat gives st_mode S_IFIFO | 0600 and zero in
       every other field.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   (fstat) fd is not an open file descriptor.
       ENOENT  (stat) path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR (stat) A component of the path prefix is not a
               directory.
       EACCES  (stat) Search permission is denied on a directory
               in the path.
       EFAULT  path or st is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               (stat) path is 256 characters or more, or a
               component is longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       chmod(2), chown(2), utime(2), ls(1), fs(5)

BUGS
       There is no lstat (there are no symbolic links), and no
       S_ISFIFO macro. The filesystem keeps no access time:
       st_atime is always st_mtime. For a regular file or directory
       st_rdev holds the number of its first data block rather than
       0. There are no st_blksize or st_blocks fields.
